<h2 id="boston-economy">What makes Boston different</h2>
<p>Boston's economy runs on brains and health care. Harvard, MIT, Boston University, Northeastern, Boston College, Tufts and dozens of smaller schools put a huge student population within a few square miles. Mass General Brigham, Boston Children's, Beth Israel Deaconess and Dana-Farber employ tens of thousands of shift workers. Kendall Square and the Seaport are packed with biotech and tech companies.</p>
<p>That mix creates three kinds of demand:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Busy, well-paid professionals</strong> in Back Bay, the South End, Brookline, Newton and Cambridge who outsource childcare, pet care, cleaning and errands.</li>
  <li><strong>Students and families</strong> who need tutoring, editing, moving help and furniture assembly — all concentrated around semester changes.</li>
  <li><strong>Companies</strong> that buy writing, design, data and research help from freelancers who understand science and higher ed.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="boston-calendar">Plan around the Boston calendar</h2>
<p>Timing matters here more than almost anywhere. Mark these on your calendar:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Late August to September 1:</strong> a huge share of Boston leases turn over at once. Movers sell out, curbs in Allston and Brighton fill with furniture, and anyone with a strong back and a cart can earn.</li>
  <li><strong>Marathon Monday (Patriots' Day, April):</strong> hotels, restaurants and rideshare surge across the city.</li>
  <li><strong>October:</strong> the Head of the Charles Regatta and fall foliage bring visitors, while families start booking winter snow service.</li>
  <li><strong>December to March:</strong> snow storms create instant shoveling demand, and indoor gigs like tutoring peak before midterms and finals.</li>
  <li><strong>Game nights:</strong> Red Sox at Fenway, Celtics and Bruins at TD Garden, and Patriots out at Gillette Stadium in Foxborough.</li>
</ul>

