    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $40–$100/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$50</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Grad students, teachers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="babysitting">2. Babysitting and nanny shifts</h3>
    <p>Two-career households with long hospital and lab hours need date-night sitters, after-school pickups and backup care. Care.com and Sittercity get you started, but neighborhood parent groups in Jamaica Plain, Somerville and the South End are where repeat families come from. CPR certification helps you charge more.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $20–$35/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$100 (CPR class)</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Students, experienced caregivers</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rideshare">3. Rideshare driving</h3>
    <p>Massachusetts drivers get a minimum pay floor for engaged time — $34.48 per hour as of January 15, 2026 — under the Attorney General's settlement with Uber and Lyft. That floor doesn't cover waiting time, so position yourself where trips come fast: Logan Airport, Seaport after work, Fenway and TD Garden after games, and Back Bay on weekend nights. See our <a href="/lyft-uber/">Lyft and Uber guide</a> for the basics.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $34.48+ per engaged hour, less expenses</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Eligible car, state background check</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Evenings, airport runs</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="delivery">4. Food and grocery delivery</h3>
    <p>Dense neighborhoods and terrible parking make Boston a natural fit for bike and e-bike delivery, especially in Back Bay, Fenway, the North End and Cambridge. Drivers do better in Brookline, Newton and Quincy. Snowy nights are slow for many couriers and busy for customers, which is when bonuses tend to appear.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $16–$24/hr before costs</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Bike, e-bike or car</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible schedules</li></ul>
